 "notes": "I've resolved 182 of the 184 names; two are left without an id, and a few rest on guesses. All the names come from the 1884 west-coast section. In most station names the type word is garbled ('t.v.', 'u.u.', 'u.H.' and so on), so I matched on the name itself.\n\nWhat you should check:\n1. **n 1032 'Ridin L.H.'** – not resolved. It's a lighthouse, but no 1884 west-coast name fits. Lizard or Bideford are possible garbles. I set kind to station with no id, which breaks your rule that a station has one id.\n2. **n 1478 'teeta 6 L.V.'** – not resolved. It's a light-vessel, possibly Breaksea or Sevenstones. Also kind station with no id.\n3. **n 835 'Malaika'** – a weak guess of Trwyn Du, reading it as a garble of 'Menai L.H.'.\n4. **n 1462 'nad Lata District'** – I read it as Lake District; Carlisle District is the other likely reading.\n5. **Names I read through OCR errors:** Flunby → Flimby (461), Grimsdale → Grinsdale (514). Haggyford (519) I couldn't identify.\n6. **Places with two possible meanings:** I took the Carlisle-area reading because the notes come from that district. Bowness (152) → Bowness-on-Solway, Orton (969) → Great Orton, the Esk (1487) → the Border Esk. Preston (1013) I assumed is Preston, Lancashire.\n7. **Nash:** 1884 lists only one Nash light, so every Nash variant, including '(H.)', '(£.)' and plain 'Nash', went to nash-point-high.\n8. **Milford:** 1884 has a single Milford row, so both Milford items went to st-anns-head rather than the separate high and low lights of 1880–81.\n9. **Carnarvon Bay u.u. (221):** the type word could be read as a lighthouse, but there is only a light-vessel there, so it went to caernarfon-bay-lv.\n10. **Carlisle District (216, 229, 470):** these cover 91 observations. I made them places, not stations, but you may want to give them their own inland category.",
